`Grid.isValid()` checks all 27 regions (9 rows, 9 columns, 9 boxes) through one loop over a precomputed region list — not three separately-written nested-loop blocks.

```java
boolean isValid() {
    for (Region region : allRegions) {
        if (!region.hasNoDuplicates(this)) {
            return false;
        }
    }
    return true;
}
```

`allRegions` is built once at construction — 9 row Regions, 9 column Regions, 9 box Regions, each just a list of 9 (row, col) coordinates. `Region.hasNoDuplicates` reads the 9 cells at those coordinates from the Grid and checks for a repeated non-empty digit — one method, reused 27 times.

A backtracking `solve()` tries a digit, recurses, and undoes on conflict — pruning as early as possible instead of filling the whole grid before checking validity.

```java
boolean solve(Grid grid) {
    Cell empty = grid.findNextEmptyCell();
    if (empty == null) return true; // fully filled, no conflicts found along the way

    for (int digit = 1; digit <= 9; digit++) {
        if (grid.canPlace(empty, digit)) {
            grid.place(empty, digit);
            if (solve(grid)) return true;
            grid.clear(empty); // undo — this branch failed
        }
    }
    return false; // no digit worked here — signal the caller to backtrack further
}
```

`canPlace` checks only the cell's three regions (row, column, box) — a cheap, local check — rather than re-validating the entire grid on every candidate digit.
